      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0",
      "observation": "The underlying system is homogeneous, first-order, linear, and constant-coefficient in one spatial dimension. The unknown q maps R × R to R^m, and A is a constant real m × m matrix. Subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "hyperbolic if the matrix A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors",
      "observation": "Hyperbolicity requires a complete set of real characteristic speeds and linearly independent eigenvectors. Every vector in R^m has a unique expansion in these eigenvectors; the corresponding eigenvalues give wave propagation speeds."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "does not hold in the classical sense",
      "observation": "At discontinuities the differential conservation law is not required to hold classically; the source identifies the integral conservation law as the formulation that continues to hold."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, opening paragraph and equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "piecewise constant with a single jump discontinuity",
      "observation": "A Riemann problem consists of the hyperbolic equation with initial data q(x,0) = q_l for x < 0 and q(x,0) = q_r for x > 0. Equation (1.11) does not assign a value at x = 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, paragraph after equation (1.11)",
